{%- comment -%}
  snippets/lada-quick-view.liquid

  The panel that opens behind the quick view button on a Lada card.

  WHAT OPENS IT. snippets/lada-product-card.liquid emits the trigger and
  owns that contract; this file only reads it:

      <button type="button" data-lada-quickview
              data-product-url="{{ product.url }}"> … </button>

  Nothing else is required of the card. An <a href> carrying the same
  attribute works too, and the href is used when data-product-url is
  missing, so a future card can make the trigger a real link and keep
  working without JavaScript.

  WHAT GOES IN IT. Nothing is duplicated from the product page. On open
  the panel asks the Section Rendering API for the product's own section

      GET /products/<handle>?section_id=<qv_section_id>

  and injects the part of it that is worth showing in a panel. That is
  the same trick assets/kj-cart.js uses for the bag: the server renders
  the markup, so prices come back formatted by `money` in the shop's
  currency and this file never owns a second copy of a buy form that can
  drift out of step with the real one.

  WHICH PART. The fetched section is searched in this order:

      [data-lada-qv-content]   the section nominates its own buy column
      .lpdp__info              sections/main-product-lada.liquid's column
      .pdp__info               the shared product page's buy column
      the whole section

  and the lead photograph in the matching order: [data-lada-qv-media],
  then .lpdp__frame img / .lpdp__img, then the shared page's gallery.
  Never a bare <img>: the retry below fetches the WHOLE page, and a
  piece with no photograph yet would match the first image on it, which
  is another product. An empty plate is the honest answer.

  So the Lada product section is already picked up by name, and a
  section that wants to nominate something else only has to put
  data-lada-qv-content on its buying column and data-lada-qv-media on
  its lead frame to jump the queue with no change here.

  WHY A SECTION ID SETTING AND NOT A CONSTANT. A section id is the KEY in
  the JSON template, not the filename: templates/product.json and
  templates/product.lada.json both call their one section "main", so
  that is the default and it is currently right for both. If a future
  template names it something else, this is a setting the client (or
  whoever builds that template) can correct without touching code — and
  even if it is wrong, the panel still fills, because a failed or empty
  section response retries against the plain product URL. Two requests
  in the worst case, none in the normal one.

  WHY A REAL <dialog>. assets/kj-cart.js hand-rolls modality for the bag
  because that drawer has to co-exist with an inert page it manages
  itself. A second panel doing the same attribute bookkeeping would have
  two scripts writing `inert` on the same elements and clearing each
  other's work: adding to the bag from inside this panel opens the bag on
  top of it, which is exactly the collision. showModal() puts this in the
  browser's top layer instead, where the focus trap, the inertness of the
  rest of the page and Escape are the browser's job and cannot fight
  kj-cart.js over an attribute. The behaviour a customer sees is the
  drawer's behaviour: Escape closes, the scrim closes, focus starts on
  the close button and comes back to the card that opened it.

  Usage, once per page, at the end of the section that renders the grid:

    {% render 'lada-quick-view',
       qv_section_id: section.settings.qv_section_id,
       close_label: section.settings.qv_close_label,
       loading_label: section.settings.qv_loading_label,
       error_text: section.settings.qv_error_text,
       full_label: section.settings.qv_full_label,
       sold_label: section.settings.sold_label,
       fallback_title: section.settings.qv_title %}

  Every string is a setting the client owns; the defaults below are only
  the fallback for a caller that has not added them yet. They are all
  sentence case. Uppercase on this panel comes from text-transform in
  the stylesheet and is never typed into a setting value, but what the
  client reads in the theme editor is the value, so a default that
  starts lowercase reads as a typo to him rather than as a style.

  Render it ONCE. It carries ids, and two of these on a page would make
  them duplicates.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign qv_section_id = qv_section_id | default: 'main'
  assign close_label   = close_label   | default: 'Close'
  assign loading_label = loading_label | default: 'Loading'
  assign error_text    = error_text    | default: 'This did not load.'
  assign full_label    = full_label    | default: 'See the full page'
  assign fallback_title = fallback_title | default: 'Quick view'

  # The only string the JavaScript ever writes into the panel. When a
  # chosen colour or size is not available the add button says this
  # instead of what it says at rest, and "sold out" is right for both
  # the piece being gone and that one size being gone. Same default as
  # the card, so pass the card's setting through and the two agree.
  assign sold_label = sold_label | default: 'Sold out'
-%}

<dialog class="lada-qv" id="lada-qv" data-lada-qv
        aria-labelledby="lada-qv-title"
        data-lada-qv-section="{{ qv_section_id | escape }}"
        data-lada-qv-sold="{{ sold_label | escape }}"
        data-lada-qv-fallback-title="{{ fallback_title | escape }}">

  {%- comment -%}
    The scrim is for the one case showModal() is not available. A modal
    dialog paints ::backdrop and this element is hidden under :modal, so
    on every current browser it is dead weight of one empty div; on a
    browser that never gets to showModal it is the only thing darkening
    the page. Written this way round on purpose: a browser that does not
    understand :modal drops that rule and keeps the scrim, which is the
    safe direction to fail.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="lada-qv__scrim" data-lada-qv-close aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="lada-qv__panel" data-lada-qv-panel tabindex="-1">

    <header class="lada-qv__head">
      {%- comment -%}
        h2, never h1. One h1 per page, and on the listing that is the
        page heading. The name here is set from the card that opened the
        panel before it opens, so the dialog has its real accessible
        name from the first frame rather than announcing "Quick view"
        and correcting itself when the fetch lands.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <h2 class="lada-qv__title" id="lada-qv-title" data-lada-qv-title>{{ fallback_title }}</h2>

      <button class="lada-qv__x" type="button" data-lada-qv-close
              aria-label="{{ close_label | escape }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
             stroke-width="1.5" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 5l14 14M19 5L5 19"/></svg>
      </button>
    </header>

    <div class="lada-qv__body" data-lada-qv-body>

      {%- comment -%}
        The quiet loading state. It is the shape of what is coming, a
        4:5 plate and three lines, not a spinner: the panel is already
        open and the plate is where the photograph will be, so nothing
        moves when the content arrives. The word itself is for screen
        readers only, because a customer looking at the panel can see it
        is loading and a second copy in print is the writing this build
        exists to remove.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="lada-qv__load" data-lada-qv-load hidden>
        <div class="lada-qv__load-plate" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="lada-qv__load-lines" aria-hidden="true">
          <span></span><span></span><span></span>
        </div>
        <p class="vh">{{ loading_label }}</p>
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        Failure, said plainly, with the way out. role="alert" on an
        element that starts hidden is the theme's existing idiom
        ([data-kj-atc-error] does the same): unhiding it announces it,
        and a panel that has already taken focus would otherwise change
        silently. Never a spinner that keeps turning.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="lada-qv__fail" data-lada-qv-fail role="alert" hidden>
        <p class="lada-qv__fail-t">{{ error_text }}</p>
        <a class="lada-qv__fail-a" href="#" data-lada-qv-full>{{ full_label }}</a>
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        Where the fetched markup lands. JS builds exactly two children
        in here, the media plate and the buying column, so the grid has
        a shape it can rely on whatever the section returns.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="lada-qv__slot" data-lada-qv-slot></div>
    </div>

    <footer class="lada-qv__foot" data-lada-qv-foot hidden>
      <a class="lada-qv__full" href="#" data-lada-qv-full>{{ full_label }}</a>
    </footer>
  </div>
</dialog>
